1
00:00:01,461 --> 00:00:07,882
Live API requires private reviewer access and consent before sending project context to OpenAI.

2
00:00:07,882 --> 00:00:12,455
Iteration 3 live demo: the deployed app uses real OpenAI API responses.

3
00:00:12,455 --> 00:00:19,871
The example is a small study tracker. Its scope fits a student web project.

4
00:00:19,871 --> 00:00:26,288
The live manager asks questions before drafting requirements.

5
00:00:26,288 --> 00:00:40,330
Every requirement needs human approval before the manager creates the task plan.

6
00:00:40,330 --> 00:00:46,880
Tasks have specialist roles, expected outputs and dependencies. Approval starts execution.

7
00:00:46,880 --> 00:01:18,404
Live specialists run in dependency order and receive completed prerequisite deliverables.

8
00:01:18,404 --> 00:01:29,752
The AI review shows findings. It cannot approve its own work or claim generated tests ran.

9
00:01:29,752 --> 00:02:07,818
Feedback sends the previous deliverable and requested change back to the selected specialist.

10
00:02:07,818 --> 00:02:18,168
The live revision produces a new deliverable. Affected outputs need fresh human approval.

11
00:02:18,168 --> 00:02:24,306
The final package includes approved deliverables and revision history. Download keeps a separate copy.

12
00:02:24,306 --> 00:02:36,760
Next iteration: assemble actual project files, run builds and tests, and provide a working preview.
